  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/b/b7/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_17.png",
  "plot_summary": "The FF wave goodbye to Ant-Man as he streaks away -- how is he flying, as I'm sure the FF do not have the catapult that he uses back at his lab?&nbsp; We find out that Reed has a \"launching platform\" - I wondered about that.&nbsp; The next page recaps their encounter with Doom in the micro world from the previous issue.\n\nReed produces a radar that's \"extra sensitive to human flesh covered by steel!\" (<i>Holy cow!&nbsp; I wonder if Iron Man shows up</i>?)&nbsp; While he's using it to search for Doom, the others begin searching in their own ways, including Ben on the street where a woman says \"He's so grotesque that he's almost <i>beautiful</i>!\" (<i>say what</i>?)&nbsp; They finally all give up - Doom is too clever to have left clues -- and Reed admits they'll have to wait for him to make the first move.\n\nThey prepare to \"continue with our normal social lives\" and attempt to slip past the throng of fans in the lobby, heading out through the freight elevator where an old janitor fan insists on shaking their hands, but it's Doom in disguise, and he's placed a tracker on each of their hands.&nbsp; He then returns to his \"hidden lab\" and sends out four \"lighter-than-air robots\" to tackle the FF individually.&nbsp; Each is shown out around town being pestered by one of them, before all four return to HQ where Reed has a hunch where they came from.&nbsp; They discover the discs and get rid of them, but it's only the first phase of Doom's plan.\n\nHe considers his brain \"the equal of Reed Richards' himself\" but admits he doesn't understand other humans, but seeing the Thing be with Alicia shows him their weak spot - even the Thing can be out amongst \"normal people\" while he hides away.&nbsp; He sends out a \"grappler ray\" to Alicia, which lifts her up through the air toward Doom, who we find is hidden high above in a cloud where his floating lab is.\n\nAlicia's floating in air makes the front page of that evening's paper, and the Thing sees her.&nbsp; Doom's face appears in the air, claiming that he is \"still human enough to wish to <i>gloat</i> over your hapless plight!\"&nbsp; He threatens that if they don't leave him alone, he will unleash disaster on the city, and he has Alicia as his trump card in the end.&nbsp; Reed doesn't care - he's going after him, but the Thing wants no part of it - Alicia must be kept safe.\n\nDoom exalts as they begin fighting amongst themselves, and we learn that he doesn't need money but only wants a \"post in the President's cabinet.&nbsp; It is only fitting that a man of my ability have at least cabinet rank in the government!\" (see note below)&nbsp; We're given a brief scene in the Oval office, where we see only the back and top half of the President's head while they discuss the demands.&nbsp; It's clearly JFK, and he excuses himself saying that \"It's Caroline's bedtime!\"\n\nDoom retaliates on the snub by beginning a one-man war.&nbsp; Everywhere machines run amok, in factories, military operations etc.&nbsp; \"Almost overnight, the mighty industrial complex of America seems to grind to a halt!\"&nbsp; We see the Russians rejoice before \"Comrade K\" tells him to cut it out - \"for all we know, he may strike <i>here</i> next!\"\n\nThe government is forced to plead for help from the FF.&nbsp; Reed has already been thinking about it.&nbsp; With his scanners, he's learned that the four robots from earlier were actually reading their atomic structures, and that now if they approach Doom's ship, he has their patterns to zero in on with his disintegrator rays.&nbsp; Reed realizes that the only one who can slip past that defense is the Thing, but only if he can become Ben Grimm, because that pattern has <i>not</i> been stored by Doom.&nbsp; After long hours, he comes up with a formula, which successfully transforms him back to human form, but for how long?\n\nBen flies toward the floating lab in a \"silent, transparent, magnetically powered, one-man plastic bubble.\"&nbsp; He successfully gets past the \"rotating disintegrators!\"&nbsp; On board the ship, he changes back to the Thing and destroys the disintegrators.&nbsp; The rest of the team is now able to join, and they rush off in separate directions to face the various menaces we saw depicted on the cover.&nbsp; But of course, they get by them, causing Doom to exclaim \"That accursed foursome has more lives than a pack of <i>cats</i>!\"&nbsp; He unleashes the \"dread fury of my hurtling power spheres!\"&nbsp; (<i>Oh no</i>!)&nbsp; He notes that he doesn't see the girl, \"but she is unimportant!\"&nbsp; (<i>Why would he expect to actually <b>see</b> the Invisible Girl anyway</i>?)\n\nThe power spheres encase the other three, and they send them into another dimension - Doom has won!&nbsp; But the three soon bust into the room, and it turns out that the Torch had cast three \"flame images\" (<i>ugh</i>) - Doom is nonplussed - he still has Alicia.&nbsp; But we learn then that Sue has gone off to find her, which is why she wasn't there.&nbsp; She switches places with Alicia and is ready for Doom when he goes to retrieve her.&nbsp; Doom and Sue battle for over a page, where we find out that Sue knows judo, being taught \"by one of the world's greatest experts: Reed Richards!\"&nbsp; (What?&nbsp; Since when would a brainiac scientist like Reed also have time to be one of the world's greatest experts on judo?&nbsp; Unlikely!)\n\nTheir fight is interrupted by the arrival of the other three, and Doom realizes that retreat is the best course of action - he jumps out of the floating lab and plummets downward out of sight.\n\nReunions all around!&nbsp; Reed and Sue!&nbsp; Ben and Alicia!&nbsp; It's enough \"mush\" to nauseate anyone, and Johnny says \"I don't <i>blame</i> Doctor Doom for <i>jumpin</i><i>'</i>!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "A far better tale than the previous issue, but still average overall.&nbsp; Doom has been featured in 5 of the 17 issues of the FF thus far (almost 30%), and each time has avoided capture.&nbsp; His characterization so far has been unsteady, and he seems more petty than later issues make him out to be, but that's not terribly surprising at this early stage in Marvel's development.&nbsp; It's obvious he's a fan favorite, or I'm sure they'd stop throwing him at us issue after issue.\n\nWe're definitely given the impression here that Doom is a citizen of the United States due to his \"modest\" terms of seeking out a seat on the President's cabinet.&nbsp; Obviously having Doom as the ruler of a small European country has not yet occurred to Lee and Kirby.&nbsp; We'll see when that plot element enters the scene - whether it's developed or sprung on us.\n\nThe story gets knocks against it for the endless barrage of whipped-up gadgets which seem to conveniently pop up whenever the story demands something.&nbsp; Examples include the radar that detects human flesh covered by steel, Doom's grappler ray and the one-man plastic bubble - the gadgets are endless!&nbsp; Reed even comes up with a ship with a giant magnet in front to cling to the outside of the floating lab!\n\nAnd having Sue face off alone against Doom shows that the co-creators are making a deliberate attempt to get her more involved.&nbsp; From the passing comments in the preceding issues and even the answers to the fan letters we had in that one recent issue, it's becoming more clear that Sue's powers need some kind of face-lift - she essentially has no offensive capability at all - and we'll be seeing that rectified soon enough...\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nFIXME - write something",
  "letters_page": "After receiving a letter from 31 year-old Harry Warren, the editors wonder who their <i>oldest</i> reader is and wonder aloud: <i>Who can top him</i>?\n\nRonn Foss closes his letter by saying:&nbsp; \"I could close by wishing long life to the MARVEL Comics Group, but I'm sure the AMAZING Mr. Lee will see to that!\"&nbsp; (<i>Heh, so true</i>...)",
  "ownership": "As for my own copy, I got one that was in roughly mid-grade condition as part of the 2000 collection for around $31, ultimately selling it on ebay for $395 in Feb, 2025, netting me over $265.",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}